('127.687.d.6.F',4,'A game that waits for the player to press a key before moving the hero is using:','["a screensaver", "event-driven design", "a static page", "batch processing"]'::jsonb,1,'Input events drive actions.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.G',3,'A game''s animation loop repeatedly:','["closes the game", "prints the code", "updates and redraws the scene", "deletes the sprites"]'::jsonb,2,'The loop produces motion.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.G',4,'If the loop runs too slowly, the player will see:','["faster music", "longer levels", "better graphics", "choppy motion"]'::jsonb,3,'Low frame rate looks choppy.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.H',3,'Using an existing game engine can save time because it:','["provides ready-made tools for graphics and input", "sells the game", "plays the game", "writes the story"]'::jsonb,0,'Engines supply common functions.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.H',4,'A team chooses an engine partly based on the platforms it supports and its:','["color", "learning curve and cost", "slogan", "box art"]'::jsonb,1,'Fit and cost matter.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.I',3,'Video display flicker happens when a screen is:','["too bright", "too small", "redrawn in a way the eye can notice", "off"]'::jsonb,2,'Visible redraws look like flicker.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.I',4,'Double buffering reduces flicker by drawing to:','["the network", "the keyboard", "the speakers", "an off-screen image first, then showing it at once"]'::jsonb,3,'The finished frame is swapped in.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.J',3,'A game''s heads-up display (HUD) shows the player things like:','["score and health", "server logs", "source code", "sales data"]'::jsonb,0,'HUDs show key status.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.J',4,'A well-laid-out screen puts the most important information where it is:','["hidden", "easy to see without blocking play", "tiny", "off screen"]'::jsonb,1,'Layout supports readability.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.K',3,'A game controller, keyboard and mouse are all:','["storage devices", "output devices only", "input devices", "power devices"]'::jsonb,2,'They send player input.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.K',4,'A game that supports both touch and gamepad controls must:','["remove the input", "use only one", "ignore both", "handle input from each type of device"]'::jsonb,3,'Code must read each device.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.L',3,'Squash and stretch, from animation principles, make movement feel more:','["lively and natural", "silent", "random", "flat"]'::jsonb,0,'These principles add life.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.L',4,'Easing a character''s start and stop so it speeds up and slows down is called:','["tile drop", "slow in and slow out", "pixel swap", "hard cut"]'::jsonb,1,'Easing mimics real motion.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.M',3,'A game chooses what an enemy does based on the player''s distance. This is:','["sound design", "packaging", "decision making in code", "level art"]'::jsonb,2,'Decisions use conditions.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.M',4,'A decision with only two outcomes, like attack or flee, is:','["an art decision", "a random decision", "a continuous decision", "a binary decision"]'::jsonb,3,'Two outcomes make a binary choice.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.N',3,'A trigger in a game is a zone that:','["starts an action when the player enters", "deletes the player", "pauses the engine", "prints a score"]'::jsonb,0,'Triggers fire events.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.N',4,'A listener in event handling:','["draws the sprites", "waits for a specific event and then runs code", "plays the music loop", "stores the score"]'::jsonb,1,'Listeners respond to events.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.O',3,'Collision detection tells the game when:','["a level loads", "a song ends", "two objects touch or overlap", "a file saves"]'::jsonb,2,'Collisions trigger reactions.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.O',4,'Checking collisions using rectangles drawn around sprites is called:','["tile shuffling", "pixel painting", "texture baking", "bounding-box collision"]'::jsonb,3,'Boxes make fast checks.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.P',3,'A tile-based game builds its world from:','["small repeating square images", "only sound", "one huge photograph", "only text"]'::jsonb,0,'Tiles tile the map.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.P',4,'A tile map is often stored as:','["a sound clip", "a grid of numbers pointing to tile images", "a movie", "a password"]'::jsonb,1,'Numbers index tiles.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.Q',3,'Artificial intelligence in a game controls how:','["the box prints", "the disc spins", "non-player characters make decisions", "the logo looks"]'::jsonb,2,'AI drives NPC behavior.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.Q',4,'A simple AI that moves an enemy toward the player each turn is using:','["menu logic", "random music", "save slots", "chase behavior"]'::jsonb,3,'Chasing follows the target.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.R',3,'Game balance means that no strategy or character is:','["unfairly stronger than all the others", "colorful", "loud", "visible"]'::jsonb,0,'Balance keeps play fair.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.R',4,'If everyone picks the same overpowered character, the designer should:','["add more music", "tune the character''s stats", "delete the menu", "ignore it"]'::jsonb,1,'Tuning adjusts values.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.S',3,'Leveling up in a game is an example of:','["file compression", "screen flicker", "player progression", "network lag"]'::jsonb,2,'Progression rewards advancement.','original'),
('127.687.d.6.S',4,'A game where levels must be finished in one fixed order uses:','["open-world progression only", "random progression", "no progression", "linear progression"]'::jsonb,3,'Linear games run in order.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.A',3,'A phone app''s buttons should be large enough for fingers, which makes the interface suited to its:','["touch screen", "keyboard only", "mouse wheel", "printer"]'::jsonb,0,'Touch targets must be finger-sized.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.A',4,'A tablet app often shows more content per screen than a phone app because the tablet has:','["fewer pixels", "a larger display", "a slower processor", "no battery"]'::jsonb,1,'Screen size changes layout.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.B',3,'A browser-based mobile app runs inside the phone''s:','["speaker", "camera", "web browser", "charger"]'::jsonb,2,'Web apps use the browser.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.B',4,'A hybrid mobile app combines web technology with:','["a spreadsheet", "a printer driver", "a modem", "a native app wrapper"]'::jsonb,3,'Hybrids wrap web code in a native shell.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.C',3,'A login screen, a menu and a map view are each a:','["component of the app", "firmware", "battery", "network cable"]'::jsonb,0,'Components are building blocks.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.C',4,'Before building a component, a developer should confirm it meets:','["the last app''s color", "an identified user need", "a random guess", "a friend''s opinion only"]'::jsonb,1,'Needs drive components.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.D',3,'Browser-based mobile apps are built mainly with HTML, CSS and:','["Fortran", "COBOL", "JavaScript", "assembly only"]'::jsonb,2,'Web technologies power browser apps.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.D',4,'One advantage of a browser-based app is that users do not need to:','["use a screen", "own a phone", "use the internet", "install it from an app store"]'::jsonb,3,'Web apps open through a link.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.E',3,'A native app is built for:','["a specific mobile platform such as iOS or Android", "every device without changes", "only printers", "only desktops"]'::jsonb,0,'Native apps target one platform.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.E',4,'A native app can often use device features such as the camera and GPS more:','["never", "directly and efficiently", "only with a browser", "slowly"]'::jsonb,1,'Native code has direct access.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.F',3,'An app that has a native shell and web-based screens is built as a:','["printed app", "pure native app", "hybrid app", "hardware app"]'::jsonb,2,'Hybrid mixes both.','original'),
('127.688.d.1.F',4,'A reason to choose a hybrid approach is to:','["skip design", "avoid all testing", "remove updates", "reuse most code on several platforms"]'::jsonb,3,'Hybrids share code across platforms.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.A',3,'A team functions best when each member knows their:','["role and responsibilities", "favorite color", "password only", "phone brand"]'::jsonb,0,'Clear roles prevent confusion.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.A',4,'A team in which members share updates regularly is practicing good:','["compression", "communication", "formatting", "encryption"]'::jsonb,1,'Communication keeps work aligned.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.B',3,'A bug nobody can fix alone gets solved when the team:','["quits", "ignores it", "shares ideas and tries solutions together", "blames one person"]'::jsonb,2,'Teamwork solves problems.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.B',4,'Pair programming, where two developers work at one computer, helps by:','["doubling battery life", "hiding code", "removing testing", "catching errors early"]'::jsonb,3,'Two sets of eyes spot mistakes.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.C',3,'A typical development workflow for a mobile app moves from planning to design, then to coding and:','["testing", "guessing", "deleting", "selling"]'::jsonb,0,'Testing follows building.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.C',4,'After release, a team continues the workflow with:','["deleting the source", "updates and maintenance", "hiding the app", "closing the team"]'::jsonb,1,'Apps need ongoing care.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.D',3,'Breaking a big app into small tasks with due dates is a:','["hardware spec", "coding language", "time-management technique", "compression method"]'::jsonb,2,'Small deadlines keep progress steady.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.D',4,'A developer who updates a task board daily is practicing:','["avoiding the team", "ignoring deadlines", "skipping testing", "tracking progress against a schedule"]'::jsonb,3,'Tracking helps meet deadlines.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.E',3,'Writing ''users forget their passwords, so the app will include a reset link'' describes a:','["problem and its solution", "bug report only", "price", "logo"]'::jsonb,0,'A problem statement paired with a fix.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.E',4,'A clear problem solution description tells what the problem is and:','["what color to use", "how the app will fix it", "where the team eats", "who owns the phone"]'::jsonb,1,'Solutions must be explained.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.F',3,'A developer records how an issue was fixed in a shared wiki so others can:','["delete it", "hide it", "reuse the solution later", "forget it"]'::jsonb,2,'Documentation spreads knowledge.','original'),
('127.688.d.2.F',4,'Sharing a solution through a short video demo is an example of using:','["only text", "only email", "only code", "different media to communicate"]'::jsonb,3,'Media choice suits the audience.','original'),
('127.688.d.3.A',3,'People who use, pay for or are affected by an app are its:','["stakeholders", "firewalls", "simulators", "compilers"]'::jsonb,0,'Stakeholders have an interest in the project.','original'),
